## Code Style & Formatting

- **Indentation**: 2 spaces, never tabs
- **Line length**: Soft wrap at 80 characters, hard limit at 120
- **Naming conventions**:
  - Functions/methods: camelCase, actions prefixed with verb (e.g., `handleClick`, `validateInput`)
  - Classes/Components: PascalCase
  - Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
  - Private methods: prefix with `_` (e.g., `_internalHelper`)
  - Boolean variables: prefix with `is`, `has`, `can`, `should` (e.g., `isLoading`)

- **Quotes**: Use single quotes for strings (unless template literals needed)
- **Semicolons**: Always use semicolons
- **Trailing commas**: Use in multi-line objects/arrays
- **Comments**: Use JSDoc for functions, inline comments for "why" not "what"

## Language & Framework Standards

### JavaScript/TypeScript
- Use `const` by default, `let` for reassignment, never use `var`
- Use arrow functions for callbacks
- Use async/await over promises when possible
- Import statements at top, grouped by: external, internal, types
- No default exports; use named exports

### React Specific
- Functional components only, use hooks
- Component naming: PascalCase file names matching component name
- Props destructuring at function signature
- Custom hooks prefix with `use` (e.g., `useCustomHook`)
- Avoid prop drilling; use Context API or state management

## Testing Requirements

- **Minimum coverage**: 80% code coverage for all files
- **Test location**: Test files in `src/__tests__/` or `*.test.ts` alongside source
- **Framework**: Jest for unit tests, React Testing Library for component tests
- **Test naming**: Describe what's being tested, not how. Use `it('should...', ...)` format
- **Requirements**:
  - Happy path tests required
  - Error/edge case tests required
  - All error throws must be tested
  - Component interactions must be tested

## Code Quality Standards

### Performance
- Bundle size critical for web: keep total under 50KB gzipped
- Individual packages: no single package > 20KB gzipped
- Lazy load code splitting for features > 5KB
- Optimize all images to WebP format when possible
- Memoize expensive calculations: use `useMemo` and `useCallback`

### Security
- Never commit secrets, API keys, or credentials
- Use environment variables for all configuration
- Validate all user input server-side AND client-side
- Escape output to prevent XSS
- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection
- CORS configuration must be restrictive
- Rate limiting on all public endpoints

### Maintainability
- Keep functions under 30 lines (prefer 10-15)
- Maximum cyclomatic complexity: 5
- Avoid nesting deeper than 3 levels
- DRY principle: don't repeat code across files
- Single Responsibility Principle: functions do one thing well
- Error messages must be user-friendly and actionable

## Git & Commits

- **Commit messages**: Use conventional commits format
  - `feat: add new feature`
  - `fix: correct bug`
  - `refactor: restructure code`
  - `test: add or update tests`
  - `docs: update documentation`

- **Branch naming**: `feature/description` or `fix/description`
- **Before committing**: Must pass lint and tests
- **No force pushes**: Unless explicitly needed for cleanup

## Documentation

- **README**: Keep updated with setup, usage, and architecture
- **Inline comments**: Explain "why", not "what"
- **JSDoc**: All exported functions/classes must have JSDoc comments
- **Types**: Prefer TypeScript types over comments
- **Complex logic**: Add explanatory comments or refactor to clear variable names

## API & Database

- **REST conventions**: Use HTTP methods correctly (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
- **Status codes**: Return appropriate HTTP status codes
- **Error responses**: Include error message and code in response
- **Validation**: Validate input at API boundary
- **Database**: Use migrations for schema changes, never manual SQL

## Accessibility & UX

- **Web**: WCAG 2.1 AA compliance minimum
- **Mobile**: Touch targets minimum 44x44 pixels
- **Forms**: Labels associated with inputs, clear error messages
- **Loading**: Show loading states for async operations
- **Responsive**: Mobile-first design approach

## Environment & Build

- **Build checks**: Must have:
  - `npm run type-check` passing (no TypeScript errors)
  - `npm run lint` passing (no ESLint errors)
  - `npm run test` passing (all tests green)
  - Bundle size within limits

- **Before committing**: Run all checks locally
- **CI must pass**: No merging with failing CI checks

## Review Checklist

Before creating a PR, the agent should verify:

- ✅ Code follows style guide
- ✅ Tests written for all changes (80%+ coverage)
- ✅ No console.log or debug statements
- ✅ Comments explain "why" not "what"
- ✅ Performance optimizations applied where needed
- ✅ Security review: no hardcoded secrets, proper validation
- ✅ All lint/type/test checks pass
- ✅ Commit messages follow conventions
- ✅ PR body explains changes clearly
- ✅ Related issues/PRs linked

### Multi-Agent Issue Checklist

When issue contains multi-agent sections, also verify:

- ✅ All Security Requirements implemented (no exceptions)
- ✅ Architecture decisions followed (file paths, patterns)
- ✅ Performance criteria met (caching, optimization)
- ✅ All test cases from TestWriter covered
- ✅ Edge cases explicitly handled
- ✅ No deviations from specified API contracts

## Special Considerations

### When Refactoring
- Preserve existing behavior unless explicitly changing it
- Add deprecation warnings before removing old code
- Update all tests to verify behavior unchanged
- Include migration guide in PR

### For Bug Fixes
- Add regression test that catches the original bug
- Document root cause in PR or comments
- Consider if bug exists elsewhere in codebase

### For Performance Work
- Include before/after metrics in PR
- Profile to verify improvements
- Ensure no correctness regressions

### For Security Changes
- Never ship security fixes to production without review
- Include security assessment in PR
- Consider backwards compatibility impact

